# g=<cos(theta)>  scattering factors for DUSTEM
#
# NOT FROM THE DustEM DISTRIBUTION.  The distribution ships no G_ file
# for this population; Guillet et al. (2018, A&A 610, A16), whose model D
# this is, computed no asymmetry parameter, and DustEM reads a G_ file
# only under its `pdr` run keyword, which this model does not set.  This
# table was computed by SEDust with
#   tmatrix/driver/spheroid_asymmetry_table.f90
# on 2026-09-08 14:53 local time.
#
# material : BE amorphous carbon (Zubko et al. 1996)
#             ../data/dielectric/index_amcBE_ZMCB96
# shape    : prolate spheroid, axis ratio b/a =  0.33333 (Guillet et al. Table 3, model D)
# geometry : random orientation.  Q and g are defined against the
#            volume-equivalent sphere radius a_V, as Guillet et al.
#            Sect. 3.6 defines them, and the radii below are a_V.
# radii    : the same grid as ../data/g18d/oprop/Q_amCBE_0.3333x.DAT
# rows     : the wavelengths of oprop/LAMBDA.DAT, in that order.
#
# METHOD, by size parameter x = 2 pi a_V / lambda:
#   x < 0.1              g = 0.  Rayleigh dipole scattering is
#                        forward-backward symmetric; the correction
#                        is O(x^2).  Exact, not an approximation.
#   0.1 <= x <= 50,      g = the random-orientation T-matrix asymmetry
#   solver converged     (Mishchenko), which is the analytic form of
#                        the 768-direction HEALPix orientation average
#                        Guillet et al. Eq. 19 takes.  Tolerance 1e-3,
#                        retried at 1e-2 and 3e-2; a retried solve is
#                        kept only where it also reproduces the
#                        published Q_abs and Q_sca to 1%.
#   otherwise            g of the VOLUME-EQUIVALENT SPHERE by Mie
#                        theory at the same m.  APPROXIMATION.  In
#                        double precision the T-matrix gives up well
#                        below the x = 50 bound (Guillet et al. ran an
#                        extended-precision build and reached
#                        a_V/lambda ~ 3 for b/a = 1/3, their
#                        footnote 4), so this covers the large-x end.
#                        MEASURED bound, from the regime-2 cells of
#                        this very table, where both forms are known:
#                          the stand-in is entered only at x >=    5.35, and over
#                          that band |g_sphere - g_spheroid| <=  0.0146 (at x =    5.36),
#                          and <=  0.0146 over the radii the model reaches.
#                        Anywhere in regime 2 the gap reaches  0.1699 (at x =    0.91),
#                        but that x is below the threshold above, so the
#                        stand-in is never reached there: the two forms
#                        part company at moderate x and converge as x
#                        grows.
# census  : 51093 Rayleigh, 24644 T-matrix, 4263 sphere entries.
# note    : the BE index table ends at 0.0503 um; m is held constant
#           from there to the 0.04 um end of LAMBDA.DAT.  The Q_ table
#           of this population is identically zero for lambda < 0.09 um,
#           so those rows carry no weight in this model.
#
# nsize (number of sampled sizes in this file)
# sizes (microns)
# g values: g(size1),...., g(sizeN)
#
